# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
bin/
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Compiled example programs. Go names a binary after its package dir, so
# building examples/a2a or examples/plugins/echo (at the repo root or in place)
# drops an extensionless `a2a`/`echo` binary. The patterns are anchored so they
# catch only the binaries, not the internal/a2a, examples/a2a, or
# examples/plugins/echo source dirs of the same name. Prefer `go build -o bin/`.
/a2a
/echo
examples/a2a/a2a
examples/plugins/echo/echo
# tools/bwpin names its binary the same way, so `go build ./tools/bwpin` leaves a `bwpin`.
/bwpin
tools/bwpin/bwpin

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# OS and tool caches. Listed here so a contributor without a global ignore
# does not commit them.
.DS_Store
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# The linter config stays tracked. A global ignore for `.golangci.yml` on a
# contributor's machine would otherwise keep it out of the checkout CI lints,
# so CI and `make lint` would run different rule sets.
!.golangci.yml
# .github/ stays tracked for the same reason: a global ignore for it would hide
# new workflows, issue forms, and CODEOWNERS from `git add`.
!/.github/

# Local agent working directories (specs, scratch, review output). They hold
# machine-specific notes and never belong in the public tree, whatever a
# contributor's global ignore says.
.claude/
.agents/
